</header>

<section class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">What it hears</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Bravo listens continuously on 1090&nbsp;MHz and decodes ADS-B
    position messages in Extended Squitter (DF17) format.
  </p>
  <div class="columns">
    <div>
      <h3>It receives.</h3>
      <p>Other aircraft fitted with ADS-B that share your altitudes and airspace. They include newer aircraft, and older ones whose owners installed a glass cockpit or a new ADS-B transponder.</p>
      <p>Bravo also receives airliners, business jets and helicopters fitted with ADS-B. You may meet them in controlled airspace around airfields and airports.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>It does not receive.</h3>
      <p>It does not receive signals from transponders without ADS-B out, or UAT, TIS-B or ADS-R signals. It does not receive ADS-L or FLARM either. A Go receives those.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band">
  <h2 class="section-title">Bravo with your phone</h2>
  <div class="columns">
    <div>
      <h3>In your app.</h3>
      <p>It sends traffic over Bluetooth as FLARM NMEA to one navigation app: SDVFR, SkyDemon or another app that supports that format.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>An alarm you feel.</h3>
      <p>Its buzzer and vibration motor warn you of approaching ADS-B traffic.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Not a transmitter.</h3>
      <p>It does not broadcast your position. To be seen in ADS-L, add a Go.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="status" class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">Where it stands</h2>
  <div class="columns">
    <div>
      <h3>The hardware.</h3>
      <p>On sale at Seeed Studio. It ships with Meshtastic, a messaging firmware that receives no ADS-B.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>The firmware.</h3>
      <p>Open source. The firmware runs on the X1. Install it by USB-C using the steps below.</p>
      <p class="note"><a href="https://github.com/fcatuhe/skyblip/issues/184">Follow it on GitHub</a></p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Updates.</h3>
      <p>Update Bravo over Bluetooth from <%= link_to "Briefing", briefing_root_path %>, the skyblip.eu web app, and approve each update with a press on Bravo.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="install" class="band">
  <h2 class="section-title">A Bravo, ready in three steps</h2>
  <ol class="steps-grid steps-grid--sequence">
    <li>
      <h3>Connect.</h3>
      <p>Hold the button, plug in the USB-C cable and keep holding until the LED turns white, about 3 seconds. A drive named <span class="mono">X1</span> appears.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Save Meshtastic.</h3>
      <p>Copy <span class="mono">CURRENT.UF2</span> from the <span class="mono">X1</span> drive to your computer first. Dragging that file back onto the drive restores Meshtastic.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Install.</h3>
      <p>Copy the .uf2 file of the latest skyBlip Bravo release from the <a href="https://github.com/fcatuhe/skyblip/releases">releases page</a> to the <span class="mono">X1</span> drive.</p>
      <p>If your aircraft has a Mode S or ADS-B out transponder, enter its ICAO address in <%= link_to "Briefing", briefing_root_path %>. This is the transponder&rsquo;s 24-bit aircraft address, written as six characters such as <span class="mono">3C6586</span>. Briefing calls it the ADS-B address. Approve the change with a press on Bravo. This keeps your own transponder out of the traffic and stops it setting off alarms. Without a transponder, leave the address blank.</p>
      <p>Briefing connects over Bluetooth. Use Chrome or Edge on a computer or Android. Safari on iPhone cannot connect.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

